{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"Read_3_e47f12f7","content":"1\t# DATA.md — the shape of your market data (generated from THIS snapshot at setup)\n2\t\n3\tEach day is one parquet file, `date=YYYY-MM-DD.parquet`, a full 1440-row minute grid\n4\twith columns `open, high, low, close, volume, trade_count`. `close` is the scored series.\n5\t`close` is the venue's 1-minute candle close (the last trade of that minute); the other\n6\tcolumns describe the same minute. For the Hyperliquid-routed assets each minute is\n7\taggregated from the venue's 1-second candles (first open, highest high, lowest low, last\n8\tclose), the same values as the venue's 1-minute candle.\n9\t`source`/`ingested_at`/`is_final` are ingestion provenance — ignore them. Semantics:\n10\t\n11\t- **`trade_count` 0** = no trade that minute: the venue prints a flat candle (open, high, low\n12\t  and close at the previous close, volume 0), and the validator scores against it. NOT market\n13\t  hours: these venues trade 24/7. Trade density varies by asset and era — see the table.\n14\t- **NaN close** = no price at all: before the market's first trade, or a hole in the feed.\n15\t- **Missing file** = before that market's first trade, or before the snapshot's first day. A\n16\t  context reaching back before an asset's first file starts with NaN days.\n17\t- Scoring drops NaN per prompt. Never interpolate: inventing prices in the gaps is how\n18\t  you fool your own validation.\n19\t\n20\tPer-asset coverage, measured from this snapshot (untraded% = share of minutes without a\n21\ttrade, flat or NaN, among the asset's real days; first/last 30d columns show density drift —\n22\tearly months of newer listings are genuinely thin, low realized volatility there is real):\n23\t\n24\t| asset | first day | last day | real days | untraded% total | untraded% first 30d | untraded% last 30d |\n25\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n26\t| AAPLX | 2025-11-21 | 2026-11-08 | 353 | 39.1% | 60.1% | 19.0% |\n27\t| BTC | 2025-08-01 | 2026-11-08 | 465 | 0.0% | 0.0% | 0.0% |\n28\t| ETH | 2025-08-01 | 2026-11-08 | 465 | 0.0% | 0.0% | 0.0% |\n29\t| GOOGLX | 2025-11-18 | 2026-11-08 | 356 | 21.5% | 23.9% | 1.9% |\n30\t| HYPE | 2025-08-01 | 2026-11-08 | 465 | 0.1% | 0.1% | 0.0% |\n31\t| NVDAX | 2025-11-12 | 2026-11-08 | 362 | 9.7% | 14.9% | 3.2% |\n32\t| SOL | 2025-08-01 | 2026-11-08 | 465 | 0.0% | 0.0% | 0.0% |\n33\t| SP500 | 2026-03-18 | 2026-11-08 | 236 | 0.9% | 2.8% | 0.2% |\n34\t| SPCX | 2026-05-17 | 2026-11-08 | 176 | 3.0% | 4.6% | 3.3% |\n35\t| TSLAX | 2025-11-13 | 2026-11-08 | 361 | 14.3% | 26.2% | 9.4% |\n36\t| WTIOIL | 2026-01-06 | 2026-11-08 | 307 | 10.4% | 58.8% | 1.4% |\n37\t| XAU | 2025-12-22 | 2026-11-08 | 322 | 9.7% | 39.2% | 1.4% |\n38\t| XRP | 2025-08-01 | 2026-11-08 | 465 | 0.0% | 0.0% | 0.0% |\n39\t\n40\t`timestamp` loads as `datetime64[us, UTC]`. An integer view of it (`.view(\"int64\")`) counts in that unit:\n41\tderive minute indices from the dtype, never by assuming nanoseconds.\n42\t\n43\t**Offline field-scores bundle** (`SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT`): scored\n44\tprompts covered **2026-07-24 → 2026-11-07**. Rank backtests outside this\n45\trange have no field to compare against — do not query the live API for more.\n46\t\n47\tPer competition: `backtest()` drops each window's first `window_days` of reward rounds (see\n48\tCAMPAIGN.md), so the first round the bundle can rank is its first scored day plus `window_days`.\n49\tThe cadence is the spacing of the prompts the validator SCORED, which is sparser than the\n50\tprompts it issues:\n51\t\n52\t| competition | scored prompts | window_days | first ranked round | scored prompts per asset |\n53\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n54\t| com-equ-24h | 2026-07-24 → 2026-11-07 | 10 | 2026-08-03 | about 24 a day (every 60 min), 2026-07-24 → 2026-10-29; about 10 a day (every 144 min), 2026-10-30 → 2026-11-07 |\n55\t| crypto-24h | 2026-07-24 → 2026-11-07 | 10 | 2026-08-03 | about 24 a day (every 60 min) |\n56\t| crypto-1h | 2026-07-24 → 2026-11-07 | 5 | 2026-07-29 | about 144 a day (every 10 min) |\n57\t\n58\tcom-equ-24h's prompts scored since 2026-10-30 start at the same times every day (UTC):\n59\t\n60\t| asset | start times |\n61\t|---|---|\n62\t| XAU | 01:45 04:09 06:33 08:57 11:21 13:45 16:09 18:33 20:57 23:21 |\n63\t| NVDAX | 02:21 04:45 07:09 09:33 11:57 14:21 16:45 19:09 21:33 23:57 |\n64\t| GOOGLX | 00:15 02:39 05:03 07:27 09:51 12:15 14:39 17:03 19:27 21:51 |\n65\t| TSLAX | 00:33 02:57 05:21 07:45 10:09 12:33 14:57 17:21 19:45 22:09 |\n66\t| AAPLX | 00:51 03:15 05:39 08:03 10:27 12:51 15:15 17:39 20:03 22:27 |\n67\t| WTIOIL | 01:09 03:33 05:57 08:21 10:45 13:09 15:33 17:57 20:21 22:45 |\n68\t| SPCX | 01:27 03:51 06:15 08:39 11:03 13:27 15:51 18:15 20:39 23:03 |\n69\t| SP500 | 02:03 04:27 06:51 09:15 11:39 14:03 16:27 18:51 21:15 23:39 |\n70\t\n71\t**Cached realized paths** (13 assets): **2026-06-20 → 2026-11-07**. Where a\n72\tprompt's slice of the price store contains NaN, scoring substitutes the validator's own\n73\tarray for that prompt. Outside this range it cannot, and the prompt is scored on the\n74\tpoints that remain — which is a smaller CRPS from a thinner reference, not a better model.\n75\t\n76\tSteps with no price change in those paths, the arrays CRPS is computed against: XAU (5 min) 11.6%, SOL (1 min) 9.4%, XRP (1 min) 7.7%, SPCX (5 min) 6.3%, AAPLX (5 min) 5.6%, BTC (1 min) 5.3%, NVDAX (5 min) 5.2%, SP500 (5 min) 5.0%, GOOGLX (5 min) 3.9%, SOL (5 min) 3.9%, TSLAX (5 min) 3.2%, XRP (5 min) 3.0%, ETH (1 min) 2.6%, HYPE (1 min) 1.3%, WTIOIL (5 min) 1.2%, BTC (5 min) 0.6%, ETH (5 min) 0.4%, HYPE (5 min) 0.3%.\n77\t\n78\t**Daylight saving.** Prices, prompts and the calendar are in UTC. The US session and the US releases\n79\tkeep New York time, and European hours keep London time, so in UTC they move an hour at each switch.\n80\tSwitches between 2025-08-01 and 2026-11-08, on the day the clock changes, with the offset after it:\n81\t\n82\t| clock | switches in the snapshot | offset on 2026-11-08 | next switch |\n83\t|---|---|---|---|\n84\t| New York | 2025-11-02 → UTC-5, 2026-03-08 → UTC-4, 2026-11-01 → UTC-5 | UTC-5 | 2027-03-14 → UTC-4 |\n85\t| London | 2025-10-26 → UTC+0, 2026-03-29 → UTC+1, 2026-10-25 → UTC+0 | UTC+0 | 2027-03-28 → UTC+1 |\n86\t\n87\tThe NYSE session, 09:30–16:00 in New York, is 13:30–20:00 UTC in summer time\n88\t(UTC-4) and 14:30–21:00 UTC in winter time (UTC-5).\n89\t\n90\t**Scheduled events** (`calendar/events.parquet`; your champion reads it at `$SYNTH_BENCHMARK_CALENDAR`):\n91\t226 events, 2025-08-01 → 2027-01-18: every date\n92\tpublished by the cutoff, upcoming ones included. Columns: `time_utc`, `event`, `scope` (`all`, or the\n93\tassets it concerns, comma-separated), `detail`, `source`. Times are scheduled New York times in UTC,\n94\tso they move an hour with daylight saving: US macro data at 08:30, FOMC statements at 14:00, the EIA\n95\tpetroleum report Wednesdays at 10:30 (holiday weeks differ). Earnings are anchored at the session,\n96\t08:00 before the open and 16:00 after the close; an NYSE closure at the 09:30 open that does not\n97\thappen, an early close at 13:00. A date published or moved after the cutoff is not here.\n98\t\n99\tEvents of each type before and after the snapshot's last day, and the other types released at the\n100\tsame minute, whose effects cannot be told apart from it there:\n101\t\n102\t| event | before cutoff | after cutoff | at the same time as |\n103\t|---|---|---|---|\n104\t| CPI | 14 | 2 | — |\n105\t| EARNINGS | 22 | 1 | — |\n106\t| EIA_PETROLEUM | 66 | 8 | — |\n107\t| FOMC | 10 | 1 | — |\n108\t| GDP | 14 | 2 | PCE 14/16 |\n109\t| NFP | 15 | 1 | RETAIL_SALES 2/16 |\n110\t| NYSE_CLOSED | 11 | 4 | — |\n111\t| NYSE_EARLY_CLOSE | 2 | 2 | — |\n112\t| PCE | 15 | 2 | GDP 14/17 |\n113\t| PPI | 14 | 2 | RETAIL_SALES 3/16 |\n114\t| RETAIL_SALES | 16 | 2 | NFP 2/18, PPI 3/18 |"}
