{"role":"assistant","content":"Paired older-window runs confirm seasonality beats the starter on all three assets. Pure empirical residuals helped all September cells but regressed on latest XAU and TSLAX; the losses concentrate in volatile weekday prompts, while gains concentrate in quiet prompts. I’m discarding the pure version and testing a Gaussian/empirical mixture to retain both core and tail calibration.","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"Edit_108_816b78d0","function":{"name":"Edit","arguments":"{\"path\":\"agent/journal.md\",\"old_string\":\"**Result.** Pending.\\n\\n**Verdict.** Pending.\\n\\n**Lesson.** Pending.\\n\",\"new_string\":\"**Result.** Latest mean CRPS: BTC 3310.280 (−0.15% versus seasonal Gaussian), XAU 2007.430 (+0.98%), TSLAX 5777.505 (+2.41%). September: BTC 2382.976 (−3.36%), XAU 1199.396 (−3.08%), TSLAX 1591.989 (−4.91%). Latest XAU empirical residuals won only 33.3% of prompts; losses were concentrated in high-CRPS weekday prompts and gains in quiet Friday/weekend prompts. BTC's small mean gain hid a negative median paired difference, so it is not treated as a clean win.\\n\\nPaired seeded-starter September results also completed the Iteration 2 check: starter BTC 2516.520, XAU 1478.323, TSLAX 2075.544. Seasonal Gaussian therefore improved those cells by 2.01%, 16.29%, and 19.34%, respectively. Seasonality passes both windows against the starter on all three assets.\\n\\n**Verdict.** Discard the pure empirical distribution: it failed the latest-window kill threshold on two of three assets despite broad September gains. Keep seasonal Gaussian as the provisional 24h model and test a mixture rather than selecting by field-relative gaps.\\n\\n**Lesson.** Raw empirical residuals match quiet-market cores but can understate volatile-weekday tails when the context mixes regimes. Mean CRPS and per-prompt gap-to-best can rank models differently when a few high-CRPS prompts dominate; the campaign objective is mean CRPS, so the mean regression controls the verdict.\\n\\n## Iteration 4 — Gaussian/empirical residual mixture\\n\\n**Hypothesis.** An equal mixture of the seasonal Gaussian and empirical residual distributions will interpolate between quiet-market core calibration and volatile-market tail coverage, improving the weaknesses identified in Iteration 3 without changing the sigma path.\\n\\n**Change.** For each 24h increment, draw from the existing empirical residual with probability 0.5 and otherwise from a standard normal. Keep all scale estimation, clipping, centering, and 1h behavior fixed. Evaluate the same six asset-window cells.\\n\\n**Result.** Pending.\\n\\n**Verdict.** Pending.\\n\\n**Lesson.** Pending.\\n\"}"}},{"type":"function","id":"Edit_109_4c0e6edb","function":{"name":"Edit","arguments":"{\"path\":\"agent/modeling.py\",\"old_string\":\"            residual = np.clip(residual - np.median(residual), -8.0, 8.0)\\n            shocks = residual[rng.integers(0, residual.size, size=(num_simulations, steps))] * sigmas\\n        else:\\n            shocks = rng.normal(size=(num_simulations, steps)) * sigmas\\n\",\"new_string\":\"            residual = np.clip(residual - np.median(residual), -8.0, 8.0)\\n            empirical = residual[rng.integers(0, residual.size, size=(num_simulations, steps))] * sigmas\\n            gaussian = rng.normal(size=(num_simulations, steps)) * sigmas\\n            shocks = np.where(rng.random((num_simulations, steps)) < 0.5, empirical, gaussian)\\n        else:\\n            shocks = rng.normal(size=(num_simulations, steps)) * sigmas\\n\"}"}}]}
