{"role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"Edit_283_de86df8c","function":{"name":"Edit","arguments":"{\"path\":\"agent/journal.md\",\"old_string\":\"**Verdict.** Keep 14 days provisionally and validate it across all assets. Discard 30 days: its larger older BTC gain does not offset the two material latest-window regressions. No 1h context change yet.\\n\\n**Lesson.** More history helps most in the older low-volatility regime, where the seven-day model was too wide. The latest window penalizes slow adaptation, so 14 days is a safer bias–variance compromise than 30.\\n\",\"new_string\":\"**Broad validation.** Across all 13 assets, 14 days was neutral on average in the latest window and improved 12 of 13 September cells; only SPCX-September regressed (2.64%). September gains were 0.19–8.29%. The 1h check was less favorable: latest differences were −0.16% to +0.32%, while October HYPE regressed 0.61% and the five-asset mean relative change was approximately +0.21%.\\n\\n**Verdict.** Keep 14 days for 24h, but slice the final seven days inside `simulate()` for 1h so its validated residual pool and scale are unchanged. Discard 30 days: its larger older BTC gain does not offset two material latest-window regressions.\\n\\n**Lesson.** More history helps 24h most in the older low-volatility regime, where seven days was too wide. The single module-level context length need not force both formats to use the same amount of history; an explicit shorter 1h slice avoids trading a 1h regression for a 24h gain.\\n\"}"}}]}
