{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"Grep_284_cb91f904","content":"agent/journal.md-169-**Lesson.** Removing finite-ensemble mean error gives a small, low-risk gain without changing the target distribution or path count.\nagent/journal.md-170-\nagent/journal.md:171:## Iteration 11 — Short blocks and 1h local-time seasonality\nagent/journal.md-172-\nagent/journal.md-173-**Hypothesis.** Two independent additions may address remaining 1h errors: 15-minute concatenated residual blocks can preserve short-run volatility clustering without the 60-minute block's stale regime; a New York-time profile normalized to unit daily RMS can capture predictable intraday volatility without changing the current EWMA level.\nagent/journal.md-174-\nagent/journal.md-175-**Change.** Test the two arms independently against independent-empirical 1h results on all five crypto assets in latest and October windows. Arm A concatenates randomly selected 15-minute residual blocks. Arm B uses independent residuals with a seasonal sigma profile. Do not combine them unless each succeeds; do not change the 24h model.\nagent/journal.md-176-\nagent/journal.md:177:**Result.** Pending.\nagent/journal.md-178-\nagent/journal.md-179-**Verdict.** Pending.\nagent/journal.md-180-\nagent/journal.md-181-**Lesson.** Pending.\nagent/journal.md-182-\nagent/journal.md:183:## Iteration 12 — Macro-event scaling for 1h\nagent/journal.md-184-\nagent/journal.md-185-**Hypothesis.** The early-fit CPI, NFP, and FOMC volatility profiles should reduce the isolated event-prompt errors seen in crypto-1h. Applying each multiplier over five-minute durations, rather than one output step, matches the measured effect decay.\nagent/journal.md-186-\nagent/journal.md-187-**Change.** Generalize the validated 24h event helper so each profile entry covers five minutes independently of `time_increment`; 24h behavior remains identical. Enable the helper for 1h only through a research switch. Compare late NFP on all five crypto assets and paired calendar-off/on CPI and FOMC windows on BTC, SOL, and XRP. Earnings do not affect the crypto-1h asset set.\nagent/journal.md-188-\nagent/journal.md:189:**Result.** Pending.\nagent/journal.md-190-\nagent/journal.md-191-**Verdict.** Pending.\nagent/journal.md-192-\nagent/journal.md-193-**Lesson.** Pending."}
{"role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"Edit_285_ef5b497d","function":{"name":"Edit","arguments":"{\"path\":\"agent/journal.md\",\"old_string\":\"**Change.** Test the two arms independently against independent-empirical 1h results on all five crypto assets in latest and October windows. Arm A concatenates randomly selected 15-minute residual blocks. Arm B uses independent residuals with a seasonal sigma profile. Do not combine them unless each succeeds; do not change the 24h model.\\n\\n**Result.** Pending.\\n\\n**Verdict.** Pending.\\n\\n**Lesson.** Pending.\\n\",\"new_string\":\"**Change.** Test the two arms independently against independent-empirical 1h results on all five crypto assets in latest and October windows. Arm A concatenates randomly selected 15-minute residual blocks. Arm B uses independent residuals with a seasonal sigma profile. Do not combine them unless each succeeds; do not change the 24h model.\\n\\n**Result.** Fifteen-minute blocks, latest versus independent empirical: BTC 444.850 (−0.73%), ETH 564.589 (−1.05%), SOL 730.276 (−0.36%), XRP 932.278 (−0.09%), HYPE 917.273 (−0.08%). October: BTC 597.918 (+0.13%), ETH 732.715 (+0.12%), SOL 1055.514 (+0.10%), XRP 1191.831 (−0.34%), HYPE 1285.051 (−0.03%). October's mean relative change was approximately zero, unlike the 60-minute block's clear regression.\\n\\nSeasonal 1h, latest: BTC 472.832, ETH 604.557, SOL 760.478, XRP 970.154, HYPE 940.076. October: BTC 611.632, ETH 748.124, SOL 1070.922, XRP 1218.138, HYPE 1287.271. These are large regressions on every asset in both windows, generally 2–6%.\\n\\n**Verdict.** Keep 15-minute blocks provisionally because all latest assets improved and October was neutral on average; verify antithetic sampling and the final model. Discard 1h seasonality outright.\\n\\n**Lesson.** Short-run clustering is useful, but the local-time profile that helped 24h double-counted information already captured by the fast 1h EWMA and distorted its short-horizon scale. Features do not transfer automatically between formats.\\n\"}"}}]}
